      "text": "To solve the given expression \\(1 - 4 + 7 - 10 + 13 - \\cdots - 46 + 49 - 52 + 55\\), we first need to identify the pattern in the sequence and then sum the series.\n\n### Step 1: Identify the Pattern\nThe sequence alternates between positive and negative terms:\n\\[1, -4, 7, -10, 13, -16, \\ldots, -46, 49, -52, 55\\]\n\n### Step 2: Determine the Number of Terms\nTo find the number of terms in the sequence, we note that each term increases by 3. The sequence starts at 1 and ends at 55. We can express the \\(n\\)-th term of the sequence as:\n\\[a_n = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 3 = 3n - 2\\]\n\nWe need to find \\(n\\) such that \\(3n - 2 = 55\\):\n\\[3n - 2 = 55\\]\n\\[3n = 57\\]\n\\[n = 19\\]\n\nSo, there are 19 terms in the sequence.\n\n### Step 3: Sum the Series\nThe series can be paired as follows:\n\\[\n(1 - 4) + (7 - 10) + (13 - 16) + \\cdots + (49 - 52) + 55\n\\]\nEach pair sums to \\(-3\\). Since there are 19 terms, we have 9 pairs and one remaining term:\n\\[\n9 \\times (-3) + 15 = -27 + 15 = -12\n\\]\nThe first term, \\(1\\), is not part of a pair but is a singleton in the sum of pairs plus the last term.\n\n### Final Answer\nThe value of the expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-12}\n\\]",
